Day 50 - Reverse Linked List II

Problem Statement:
Given the head of a singly linked list and two integers left and right where left <= right, reverse the nodes of the list from position left to position right, and return the reversed list.

Code
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* reverseBetween(ListNode* head, int left, int right) {


        if (head == NULL || left == right)
            return head;


        ListNode dummy(0);
        dummy.next = head;


        ListNode* prev = &dummy;


        for (int i = 1; i < left; i++) {
            prev = prev->next;
        }


        ListNode* leftNode = prev->next;
        ListNode* curr = leftNode;
        ListNode* reversePrev = NULL;


        for (int i = 0; i <= right - left; i++) {
            ListNode* next = curr->next;


            curr->next = reversePrev;
            reversePrev = curr;
            curr = next;
        }


        prev->next = reversePrev;
        leftNode->next = curr;


        return dummy.next;
    }
};





Time Complexity:
O(n)

Space Complexity:
O(1)

Approach:
This solution uses in-place linked list reversal. A dummy node is created before the head to handle cases where left = 1. First, prev is moved to the node immediately before position left. The nodes from left to right are then reversed using the standard three-pointer technique. leftNode stores the original first node of the section because after reversal it becomes the last node of that section. After reversing, prev->next is connected to the new beginning of the reversed section, and leftNode->next is connected to the remaining part of the list. No new list nodes are created, so the reversal is performed in-place.
